{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed262781723/14/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"28a6681f\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nSimulate water pixels falling under gravity into basins formed by colored obstacles.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = 0. Water pixels = color 1 (blue).\n- Other colors form static solid structures (walls, steps, containers).\n- Water starts in unstable positions above or beside basins.\n\nRule:\n- Each water pixel falls straight down through bg cells.\n- When blocked below, it slides diagonally through the unique open side cell.\n- When resting on a surface, it slides horizontally toward the nearest gap where it can fall again.\n- Process repeats until no water pixel moves.\n\nKey insight: Water seeks the deepest reachable basin via diagonal and horizontal sliding, not just vertical fall.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"bc93ec48\" lines=\"48-63\">\n<description>\nCopy each foreground object anchored in a true grid corner clockwise into the next corner.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- Foreground objects are monochromatic 4-connected components.\n- Relevant objects are exactly those whose bounding box touches two adjacent borders at one grid corner.\n\nRule\n- Start from the input unchanged.\n- For each corner object, copy its pixel mask and color to the next corner clockwise: top-left to top-right, top-right to bottom-right, bottom-right to bottom-left, and bottom-left to top-left.\n- Preserve each object's orientation by aligning its bounding box to the destination corner; stamps may overwrite existing pixels at the destination, but source pixels are not cleared.\n\nKey insight\n- Interior and side-only objects are distractors; only components actually anchored to a corner participate in the clockwise perimeter copy.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"df978a02\" lines=\"57-72\">\n<description>\nLet the largest center-pointing object grow outward while every other object loses its inward tip.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a single dominant background color and four 8-connected monochromatic foreground objects.\n- The objects surround a central background gap and point toward it from cardinal directions.\n- Exactly one object has the largest area.\n\nRule\n- Find the central background gap by minimizing aggregate distance to the four objects.\n- Recolor the nearest-to-center pixel of each non-largest object to background.\n- Add a three-pixel cap in the largest object's color on the side away from the center, centered on the shared row or column.\n\nKey insight\n- The edit is a transfer of prominence around a common center: losers retract at their tips, and the unique largest object extends outward from the center.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"58f5dbd5\" lines=\"20-34\">\n<description>\nCarve color-matched motifs into solid blocks and pack them into a compact grid.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Several solid 5×5 monochrome blocks, each a distinct non-bg color.\n- For each block color, a small same-colored motif sprite elsewhere on the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Match each motif to its same-colored 5×5 block.\n- Center the motif within the block and recolor those cells to bg (punching holes).\n- Pack all carved blocks into an output grid preserving their original row/column order, separated by 1-cell gaps and a 1-cell outer border.\n\nKey insight: The motif acts as a stencil — it's not stamped onto the block but carved out of it as background holes.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"fe45cba4\" lines=\"72-89\">\n<description>\nMove the left-edge fragment into its same-colored anchor fragment to complete a solid rectangle.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background color.\n- Exactly one foreground color is split into two 4-connected objects: one\n  touches the left edge, and the other is an anchor away from the left edge.\n- The translated left-edge object and the anchor can form one filled rectangle.\nRule\n- Find the unique split color whose left-edge object can be translated onto\n  background cells beside the other same-colored object so their union is a\n  solid axis-aligned rectangle.\n- Clear the left-edge object's original cells to the background.\n- Paint that object at the unique completing offset, preserving all other cells.\nKey insight\n- The active color is identified by object structure and rectangle completion,\n  not by a fixed color id; the same-colored anchor object stays in place.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1be83260\" lines=\"41-58\">\n<description>\nDecode the colored slot map inside the marked motif and rebuild the motif lattice with those colors.\n\nInput structure:\n- A blank background surrounds a rectangular lattice of identical motifs.\n- The motif rows and columns are separated by one background line.\n- One motif has colored marker pixels in its internal odd-row/odd-column slots.\n\nRule:\n- The motif size is 2R+1 by 2C+1, where R by C is the motif lattice size.\n- Read the marked motif's odd internal slots as the color map for the R by C output motifs.\n- Slots that still contain the repeated motif color map to that repeated motif color.\n- Rebuild the compact lattice on a canvas filled with the repeated motif color,\n  painting each motif mask with its decoded color.\n\nKey insight:\n- The holes and filled odd slots inside one motif are a miniature legend for recoloring the entire repeated motif lattice.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"1ae2feb7\" lines=\"30-45\">\n<description>\nExtend row bars periodically across the opposite side of a separator column.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. A near-solid vertical separator divides the grid into two sides.\n- One side contains horizontal colored bars; the other side is blank target space.\n- Bars may be stacked by row and may sit at different distances from the separator.\n\nRule:\n- Identify the separator as the column whose non-bg cells are one color.\n- Treat the denser side as the bar side and collect each contiguous row run.\n- Process bars from nearest to farthest from the separator; for each bar, paint its\n  color every bar-length cells into the blank side without overwriting earlier paint.\n\nKey insight: Each bar's length is the period for its extension, and nearer bars claim target cells first.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"7e4d4f7c\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nCompress the L-shaped cue to three rows and add a pink copy of the top-row foreground mask.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background color, a two-color top row, and all\nforeground pixels below the top row lie in the first column as vertical markers.\n\nRule\nKeep the input top row as output row 0 and the first marker row as output row 1.\nOutput row 2 is background except at the top-row foreground columns, which are\nrecolored pink.\n\nKey insight\nThe lower repeated marker rows only identify the vertical cue; the generated\nrow is a recolored mask of the horizontal cue in the top row.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"67e490f4\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nFill holes inside a large template frame using shape-matched outside sprites.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. One large monochrome frame object (template).\n- Inside the frame: holes of various shapes (non-frame-color regions).\n- Outside the frame: small monochrome sprites scattered on bg.\n\nRule:\n- Crop to the frame's bounding box; fill entirely with frame color.\n- For each hole, find an outside sprite whose shape matches (rotation/flip allowed).\n- Fill the hole with the matched sprite's color. If multiple sprites match, use the most frequent color.\n\nKey insight: Sprites act as shape-coded color keys — their shape identifies which hole they fill, their color determines the fill color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7b80bb43\" lines=\"8-21\">\n<description>\nRemove diagonal staircase noise and complete broken straight lines.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background; one foreground color fg forming axis-aligned lines.\n- Some fg pixels form 45° diagonal chains (8-connected but 4-disconnected from lines).\n\nRule:\n- Delete all diagonal-only fg pixels (those with no orthogonal fg neighbor, only diagonal ones).\n- Fill short gaps in horizontal/vertical lines where a deleted diagonal was nearby.\n- Prune remaining diagonal-adjacent leaf pixels near former diagonal sites.\n\nKey insight: Diagonal pixels are hints marking where a straight line is broken; their span sets the maximum gap size to fill.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"c4d1a9ae\" lines=\"28-48\">\n<description>\nFill each vertical foreground band's background cells with the color of the next band to its right.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- Each other color occupies a separate vertical band defined by that color's\n  minimum and maximum columns.\n- Within a band, all cells are either that band's color or background.\n\nRule\n- Order the bands from left to right.\n- In each band, replace background pixels with the color of the next band in\n  that order, wrapping from the rightmost band back to the leftmost.\n- Preserve all existing foreground pixels and all background pixels outside\n  the bands.\n\nKey insight\n- The column bands form a cyclic palette legend: a band's gaps are completed\n  by borrowing the color from its right-hand neighbor, not by changing the\n  band's existing pixels.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a25697e4\" lines=\"9-23\">\n<description>\nDock movable tools into border-anchored frames by filling cavities.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Border-touching components cluster into anchor frames with interior bg cavities.\n- Non-border 2-color components are movable tools.\n\nRule:\n- Match each tool to a frame whose cavity size equals one of its color-region counts.\n- Rotate/flip the tool so one color fills the cavity exactly; the other stays outside.\n- If the docking orientation uses a reflection, swap the tool's two colors.\n\nKey insight: Cavity size determines tool–frame pairing; reflection triggers color swap.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e729b7be\" lines=\"8-20\">\n<description>\nComplete the scene by adding the point-reflected copy of the colored object across the central marker.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has odd dimensions, a dominant background, and a center-row marker color appearing only at the left edge, center, and right edge.\n- A single divider color fills the center column except for the center marker.\n- All remaining foreground pixels form the object to copy, entirely on the left side of the divider.\nRule\n- Keep the input unchanged.\n- For every object pixel, paint the pixel rotated 180 degrees around the center marker with the same color.\nKey insight\n- The center marker is the rotation center; the yellow-looking divider is not mirrored, it only separates the source from the empty target side.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2b9ef948\" lines=\"51-65\">\n<description>\nMove the yellow 3x3 frame by the displacement encoded by the gray path, then draw its diagonal yellow corner rays.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
